   "note": "The root box itself is right: Pokorny p. 681 prints \"1. leu-, *leuǎ : lū- ,Schmutz, beschmutzen'; lu-to- ,Kot'\", and Watkins has \"leu-2. Dirt; to make dirty\" with the same page reference. The page number POK:681 is exact. TWO CROSS-CUTTING PROBLEMS. (a) Pokorny's article contains NO Indo-Iranian member at all — only Gk. λῦμα/λύθρον, Alb. lum, Lat. polluō/lutum/lustrum, OIr. loth, Lith. lutynas. The whole Iranian branch of this chart (nodes 1-3) rests on Horn no. 42 (1893), which Horn himself calls \"durchaus unsicher\"; today Cheung (EDIV 2007, s.v. *dauH2) and Wiktionary derive آلودن from Proto-Iranian *ā-dauH- 'to smear', and پالودن from something else again. (b) The Greek branch (nodes 4-7) belongs to the HOMONYMOUS root: Pokorny's \"2. leu- ,abschneiden, trennen, loslösen'\", which happens to begin on the very same page 681 — that is almost certainly how the two got merged. Watkins keeps them apart as leu-1 'To loosen, divide, cut apart' (→ Gk. luein → DIALYSIS) and leu-2 'Dirt' (→ Lat. polluere, lutum), and Etymonline does the same. So the root as drawn is correct but over-populated.",
