     "derivation": "These are not attested Avestan or Old Persian words. They are Abolghasemi's reconstructed Old-Iranian (ایرانی باستان) forms, printed with asterisks in Fe'l-hā-ye Fārsi-ye Dari p. 26 under آلودن: past stem آلود < MP ālūd < OIr. *ā-rū-ta-, present stem آلای < MP ālāy < OIr. *ā-rāw-aya- (Nourai's \"aya\" is simply the causative suffix -aya- split off the second form), from a root *rū- glossed \"آلوده بودن\". Nourai reproduces them faithfully but relabels them \"Avestan / Old Persian\", which they are not: Bartholomae's Altiranisches Wörterbuch has no Avestan root rū-/rav- 'to be dirty', and Kent's Old Persian lexicon has nothing either. Modern Iranian etymology reconstructs the same two Persian stems quite differently, with *d not *r: Cheung EDIV s.v. *dauH2 'to smear, rub (on)' gives YAv. fraδauu- 'to be rubbed off with' (Bartholomae ³dav-, col. 688, \"nur mit fra 'reinigend abreiben'\"), MMP *'rwdg 'pollution, dirt', Sogd. δʾaw 'to smear', Khwar. δʾaw-, Oss. dawyn, and NP ālūdan/ālāy- (+ *ā-) beside andūdan (+*ham-) and zadūdan (+*uz-), the -l- of ālūdan being secondary. The chart's forms are therefore a 19th/20th-century reconstruction that current scholarship rejects.",
